   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the tax law, in relation to the authority of counties and cities to impose sales and compensating use taxes pursuant to the authority of article 29 of such law; and to repeal certain provisions of section 1210, section 1210-E and section 1224 of such law relating thereto",
    "basePrintNo": "S6566",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6566-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§1210, 1224, 1262-g, 1262-r & 1262-s, rpld §1210 sub¶¶ (iii) & (iv), rpld §1210-E, add §§1262-u & 1265, Tax L",
    "lawSection": "Tax Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6566\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the tax law, in relation to the\nauthority of counties and cities to impose sales and compensating use\ntaxes pursuant to the authority of article 29 of such law; and to\nrepeal certain provisions of section 1210, section 1210-E and section\n1224 of such law relating thereto\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThis bill would amend the Tax Law to make permanent the authority for\ncounties to impose their current additional rates of sales and\ncompensating use taxes for two-year periods, thus obviating the need\nfor counties to obtain approval through the State legislative process\nevery two years.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 of the bill would repeal subparagraph (i) of the opening\nparagraph of Tax L. § 1210 and replace it with a new subparagraph (i)\nthat recodifies the current additional rates of sales and compensating\nuse tax authorized to be imposed in. New York City and certain\ncounties, and makes the authority of those counties to impose such\nadditional rates permanent. New York City's authorization to impose an\nadditional rate is permanent in current law.\n\nSection 2 of the bill would repeal subparagraph (ii) of the opening\nparagraph of Tax L. § 1210 and replace it with a new subparagraph (ii)\nthat recodifies the current additional rates of sales and compensating\nuse tax authorized to be imposed by certain cities and preserves the\nduration of that authority under current law.\n\nSection 3 of the bill would repeal subparagraphs (iii) and (iv) of the\nopening paragraph of Tax L. § 1210 and replace it with a new\nsubparagraph (iii) to maintain the provision in existing law that\nprovides that the maximum rates imposed in Tax L. § 1224 relating to\nprior rights and pre-emption (where cities and county levy the same\ntax) shall be calculated without reference to the additional rate of\nsales and compensating use tax authorized for counties, with the\nexception of the counties of Cayuga, Cortland, Fulton, Madison, and\nOtsego, and for the cities of Mount Vernon, New Rochelle, Oswego,\nWhite Plains, and Yonkers.\n\nSection 4 of the bill would amend Tax L. § 1210 by adding a new\nsubdivision (p) that would require the counties authorized to impose\nan additional rate of sales and compensating use tax pursuant to the\nprovisions in the bill to adopt such tax by local law, ordinance, or\nresolution by a majority vote of its governing body for a period not\nto exceed two years.\n\nSection 5 of the bill would repeal Tax L. § 1210 -E. Under current\nlaw, Herkimer County is authorized to impose an additional rate of 1%\nunder clause (19) of the opening paragraph of Tax L. § 1210, and an\nadditional rate of tax of '4% under Tax L. § 1210 -E, subject to the\nsame provisions as the 1% rate. The County's current authority of 1\n14% is consolidated in bill section 1, rendering § 1210 -E\nunnecessary.\n\nSection 6 of the bill would repeal subdivisions (d), (e), (f), (g),\n(h) ,(i), (j), (k), (1), (m), (n), (o), (p), (q), (r), (s), (t), (u),\n(v), (w), (x), (y), (z), (z-1), (aa), (bb), (cc), (dd), (ee), (ff),\n(gg), and (ii) of Tax L. § 1224. These subdivisions relate to the sole\nright of certain counties and cities to impose additional rates of\nsales tax without preemption, which are recodified by bill section 7.\n\n\nSection 7 of the bill would amend Tax L. § 1224 by adding subdivisions\n(d), (e), and (f) to recodify the sole right of counties and cities\nauthorized by this bill to impose an additional sales and compensating\nuse fax without pre-emption. Provided, however, the cities of Auburn,\nCortland, Gloversville, Johnstown, Oneida, and Oneonta, may pre-empt\nany sales and compensating use tax imposed by the county in which it\nis located.\n\nSections 8, 9, 10, and 11 of the bill would amend Tax L. §§ 1262-g,\n1262-r, 1262-s, and 1262-u to, for the counties of Oneida, Ontario,\nClinton, Herkimer, recodify and conform the distribution of the amount\nof the sales and compensating use tax to be used for county or\neducational purposes to the provisions of this bill.\n\nSection 12 of the bill would provide that any reference to net\ncollections or revenue imposed by a county or city in a local law,\nordinance or resolution adopted under Tax L. Art. 29 that refers to a\nprovision repealed by this bill is deemed to be a reference to the\ncorresponding provision in this bill.\n\nSection 13 of the bill would make the bill effective immediately.\n\n STATEMENT IN SUPPORT :\n\nThis bill would amend Article 29 of the Tax Law to make permanent the\nauthority for counties to impose their current additional rates of\nsales and use taxes and would obviate the need for counties to obtain\napproval through the State legislative 'process every two years.\nCurrently, nearly all counties are authorized to impose an additional\nrate of sales and use taxes for a two-year period that expires\nNovember 30, 2017.\n\nEach county that is currently authorized to impose the additional rate\nwould still have to adopt a local enactment by a majority vote of its\ngoverning body every two years to impose its additional rate above 3%.\n\nThis bill would not change the additional rates currently authorized\nfor counties. It would also not change current laws that require\ncounties to adopt a home rule message and obtain approval through the\nState legislative process before they may increase sales and\ncompensating use taxes above their current additional tax rate.\n\nFinally, the bill would amend and recodify other related provisions of\nArticle 29 of the Tax Law to conform them to the above described\nprovisions.\n\n BUDGET IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately.\n",
